\chapter{Option, Result, and propagation}
\label{ch:30}
\index{Option, Result, and propagation}
\index{Cleanup!Result propagation}

The pervasive environment supplies canonical \OnxCodeTerm{Option<T>} and \OnxCodeTerm{Result<T,E>} declarations and copy-safe helper functions. Use Option for optional presence and Result for success with a typed error. On the admitted nominal Result, postfix \OnxCodeTerm{?} evaluates once, yields the success payload, or returns the active error to a compatible enclosing function after cleanup. For local recovery, use a match with both cases.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=2,lastline=13]{../../on1x/examples/pervasive_options.on1x}
Which error should $-3$ produce? It is both negative and odd, but
the first return wins: the caller gets error 1. With 3 the first test
passes over the return and the second gives error 2. With 82 neither
return is taken and the success payload is 41.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=15,lastline=23]{../../on1x/examples/pervasive_options.on1x}
On the 82 path, \OnxCodeTerm{half} is an integer, not a Result.
The mapping function adds one and \OnxCodeTerm{answer} returns
\OnxCodeTerm{Ok(42)}. On either error path, execution never reaches
the binding of \OnxCodeTerm{adjusted}. Propagation needs the same
nominal Result declaration and the same error type in the caller;
the caller can compute a different success type. An Option has no
such error payload to forward.

Now imagine that the success payload is the unique list from
\cref{ch:26}. Extracting it twice would give two owners of one root.
Here \OnxCodeTerm{pending?} consumes the whole result even though the
spelling does not include \OnxCodeTerm{move}. Use an explicit variant
constructor for an owner-bearing result; the generic pervasive helpers
assume copy-safe payloads. \Cref{app:ownership} follows the active
payload across two function returns and shows what the checker says
about a second extraction.

\section{Where would a fallback take effect?}
Trace the zero in the call to \OnxCodeTerm{option\_unwrap\_or}. On the
success path the code explicitly wraps the half in Some and mapping
over it keeps it present. Thus zero is never selected there. Nor does
it stand in for either error code: both errors leave the function before
it constructs an Option. If the application needs a default answer for
odd input but wants to report negative input, put that decision at a
match on the Result. Inspect the error byte in the Err arm; choosing
a fallback for an absent Option later in the pipeline cannot recover a
path that has already returned.

